The code statements that lead to the bug are:
cpu = cpumask_next(rd->rto_cpu, rd->rto_mask);
rd->rto_cpu = cpu;
if (cpu < nr_cpu_ids)
    return cpu;
Reason:
The function `rto_next_cpu()` does not check whether the selected CPU is the currently executing CPU. When the local CPU is overloaded and remains in the `rto_mask`, it can be returned again, causing a self-IPI. This, combined with other CPUs incrementing `rd->rto_loop_next`, resets the search from -1 and allows the local CPU to be reselected repeatedly, leading to an infinite self-IPI loop and hardlockup.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
if (cpu == this_cpu)
    continue;
Reason:
By adding a check to skip the current CPU (`this_cpu`), the function avoids returning the local CPU as a target for IPI-based RT push. This prevents self-IPIs and breaks the infinite loop that caused CPU hardlockup.

